#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1320.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097774} :activity/id #uuid "acc24286-334e-50b7-b51f-39796fa9c946" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050521 :topic/id #uuid "5036af57-460d-5886-bb25-967e885ae634" :topic/math-academy-id 1140} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097774 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097927 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\int x\\ln{x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\,.\n$$\n\nThe method of integration by parts can help us solve this problem. Let's remind ourselves of the \"by-parts\" formula,\n\n$$\n\\int u v' \\textrm{d}x = uv - \\int v u'\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe usually let $u = x,$ but that would leave us with $v' = \\ln(x)$ which is hard to integrate. So instead, we're going to choose our $u$ and $v'$ slightly differently, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the formula of integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{u}\\cdot {{x}_{⏟}}_{{v}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen we apply integration by parts to integrals containing logarithmic functions, we should *always* identify the logarithmic function with $u,$ not with $v'.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dbf22d18-c638-527f-a085-8fcc19fbfbeb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 897 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e5d3fd98-fbd8-5b6d-a250-876573acf24e" :step/math-academy-id 618388 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097775}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097775 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096834 :knowledge-point/canon